        "# Lesson 12: Asyncio Basics\n",
        "**Module 3: Concurrency**\n",
        "\n",
        "## Overview\n",
        "This advanced lesson covers async/await, event loop, and coroutines. You'll learn expert-level techniques and best practices used in production environments.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Topics Covered\n",
        "- Theoretical foundations\n",
        "- Advanced implementation techniques\n",
        "- Performance considerations\n",
        "- Real-world use cases\n",
        "- Industry best practices\n",
        "- Complex exercises\n",
        "\n",
        "## Prerequisites\n",
        "- Completion of Intermediate course\n",
        "- Strong Python fundamentals\n",
        "- Experience with OOP and functional programming\n",
        "\n",
        "---"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
"## Introduction\\n",
"\\n",
"In this lesson we will learn the core building blocks of **asyncio** in Python:\n",
"\n",
"- Why asynchronous I/O is useful compared to threads\n",
"- The event loop and how it schedules coroutines\n",
"- The `async` / `await` syntax\n",
"- Creating and running simple coroutines with `asyncio.run`\n",
"- Running multiple tasks concurrently with `asyncio.gather`\n",
"- Using `asyncio.sleep` as a stand-in for real I/O (HTTP calls, DB queries, etc.)\n",
"\n",
"You'll gradually move from a synchronous example to an asynchronous version and measure the performance difference."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {},
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
"import asyncio\\n",
"import time\\n",
"\\n",
"def fetch_sync(name: str, delay: float) -> None:\\n",
"    \"\"\"Simulate a blocking I/O-bound operation.\"\"\"\\n",
"    print(f\\"[sync] start {name}\\")\\n",
"    time.sleep(delay)\\n",
"    print(f\\"[sync] end   {name}\\")\\n",
"\\n",
"async def fetch_async(name: str, delay: float) -> None:\\n",
"    \"\"\"Simulate a non-blocking I/O-bound operation using asyncio.sleep.\"\"\"\\n",
"    print(f\\"[async] start {name}\\")\\n",
"    await asyncio.sleep(delay)\\n",
"    print(f\\"[async] end   {name}\\")\\n",
"\\n",
"def run_sync_example() -> None:\\n",
"    start = time.perf_counter()\\n",
"    fetch_sync(\\"A\\", 1)\\n",
"    fetch_sync(\\"B\\", 1)\\n",
"    elapsed = time.perf_counter() - start\\n",
"    print(f\\"Synchronous total time: {elapsed:.2f}s\\")\\n",
"\\n",
"async def run_async_example() -> None:\\n",
"    start = time.perf_counter()\\n",
"    await asyncio.gather(\\n",
"        fetch_async(\\"A\\", 1),\\n",
"        fetch_async(\\"B\\", 1),\\n",
"    )\\n",
"    elapsed = time.perf_counter() - start\\n",
"    print(f\\"Asynchronous total time: {elapsed:.2f}s\\")\\n",
"\\n",
"print(\\"Run run_sync_example() and then:\\")\\n",
"print(\\"  asyncio.run(run_async_example())\\")\\n"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "---\n",
"## Core Concepts\\n",
"\\n",
"At the heart of asyncio are three concepts:\n",
"\n",
"1. **Event loop** – runs your asynchronous tasks and callbacks.\\n",
"2. **Coroutines** – functions defined with `async def` that can be awaited.\\n",
"3. **Awaitables** – objects that can be used with `await` (coroutines, Tasks, Futures).\\n",
"\n",
"Key rules:\n",
"- `await` can only be used inside `async def`.\\n",
"- CPU-bound work should not run directly inside an async coroutine (use executors).\\n",
"- Use `asyncio.run(coro())` as the high-level entrypoint for your program.\\n"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {},
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
