        "---\n",
"## Best Practices & Pitfalls\\n",
"\\n",
"### Best Practices:\n",
"- Prefer `asyncio.run()` as the main entrypoint instead of manually managing loops.\\n",
"- Keep coroutines small and focused; compose them with `gather` or helper functions.\\n",
"- Always handle cancellation and timeouts for network operations.\\n",
"- Use high-level client libraries that expose async APIs when possible.\\n",
"\\n",
"### Common Pitfalls:\n",
"- Mixing blocking I/O (e.g., `time.sleep`, blocking DB drivers) inside async code.\\n",
"- Forgetting to `await` an async function (you'll get a coroutine object, not a result).\\n",
"- Starting many background tasks and never awaiting/cancelling them.\\n",
"- Swallowing `CancelledError` without cleaning up resources."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "---\n",
"## 💡 Key Takeaways\\n",
"\\n",
"- Asyncio is designed for **I/O-bound** concurrency, not CPU-heavy work.\\n",
"- The event loop schedules **coroutines** which you write with `async def` and `await`.\\n",
"- `asyncio.gather` is the main tool for running many coroutines concurrently.\\n",
"- You can model real I/O using `asyncio.sleep` while learning and benchmarking.\\n",
"- Always be mindful of blocking calls inside async code and handle timeouts/cancellation properly.\\n",
"\\n",
"---"
      ]
